{"summary":"Accept all four amendments. The first is a worthwhile consistency/maintainability fix that preserves the documented weighting contract, and the other three identify real regressions around stage-structured shrimp configuration: scenario materialization ignores legacy/new lifecycle tuning, save migration fails to preserve legacy maturation schedules, and load-time invariants do not validate shrimp runtime parameters.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-206e00d0","decision":"accept","rationale":"`add_reserve_by_feeding_units()` is documented to reuse the same feeding weights as `feeding_units()`, but it hardcodes the adult weight instead of using `ADULT_FEEDING_WEIGHT`. That is behaviorally identical today only because the constant is `1.0`; using the constant removes a silent divergence hazard and makes the implementation match its contract."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-03f6968a","decision":"accept","rationale":"This is a real configuration bug. Scenario materialization still loads `process_params.shrimp_juvenile_maturation_days`, but stage promotion now reads only `state.shrimp_params`, so that tuning is ignored. Separately, the new stage-structured knobs used by shrimp runtime logic are all populated from `ShrimpRuntimeParams::default()` because `ShrimpPreset` does not expose them, so preset/scenario data cannot control those lifecycle behaviors and defaults are silently used instead."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-b4c84d14","decision":"accept","rationale":"The migration preserves the animal-state shape change but does not preserve legacy maturation semantics. Older saves can carry a non-default `process_params.shrimp_juvenile_maturation_days`, yet after load the runtime consults only `shrimp_params.juvenile_to_subadult_days` and `subadult_to_adult_days`, which are missing and therefore default to `30` and `20`. That means old saves can change lifecycle timing on load, so migration is incomplete."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-b484d552","decision":"accept","rationale":"`SaveFile::into_engine()` relies on invariant enforcement for load-time validation, but `validate_invariants_inner()` never checks `state.shrimp_params`. As a result, malformed current-schema saves can inject impossible species settings and still load; several runtime paths then clamp or sanitize those values (`max(1.0)`, probability clamps, etc.) instead of rejecting them. That is a real robustness gap in the validation boundary."}]}